Do not introduce React, Vue, Inertia, Livewire, or another frontend framework
without an explicit architecture decision.

## Blade

Blade is presentation-oriented.

Do not put business rules into Blade templates.

Use escaped output by default.

Explicitly justify any raw HTML output.

## Alpine

Use Alpine for localized UI state and interaction.

Do not duplicate server-side authorization in Alpine.

Client-side behavior improves UX but never replaces server-side security.

## JavaScript

Use Vanilla JavaScript where it is clearer or reusable.

Avoid unnecessary client-side application complexity.

Use the Datatable for easy sorting of record


## Tailwind

Use the project's established design system and Tailwind conventions.

Avoid arbitrary one-off styling when an existing component or utility pattern
exists.

## Accessibility

Target WCAG 2.1 Level AA where specified.

Consider:

- semantic HTML
- keyboard navigation
- focus management
- labels
- accessible error messages
- color contrast
- screen reader behavior
- reduced motion
- form accessibility
- map accessibility alternatives

## Forms

Server-side validation is authoritative.

Validation errors should be accessible and understandable.

## Public vs Admin

Public interfaces and admin interfaces have different security and usability
requirements. views/public/* => controllers/public/*, views/admin/* => controllers/admin/*

Do not accidentally expose administrative functionality through public UI.


